# Everything checks out with LF, and the rule is load-bearing rather than
# cosmetic.
#
# `20260820120100_seed_settings.sql` seeds `settings.base_instructions` with a
# multi-line SQL string literal, and `db::settings::DEFAULT_BASE_INSTRUCTIONS`
# pins the same four sentences in Rust — asserted **byte for byte**, because
# "restore the default" in Settings has to produce exactly what a first launch
# did. A checkout that gave the migration CRLF would seed `\r\n` where the Rust
# constant has `\n`, and the two would differ in a way nothing but that test can
# see.
#
# Found by task 022's CI matrix, which is the first time this repository's tests
# ran on Windows at all.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Recorded CLI fixture streams (crates/core/tests/fixtures/cli/*.jsonl) are byte-for-byte
# captures of real `claude` CLI output. Line-ending normalization would silently corrupt
# them, so treat *.jsonl as opaque binary data for git's purposes.
#
# **After the wildcard above, not before**: the last matching pattern wins, so a
# `*` rule placed below this one would take the fixtures back.
*.jsonl -text
*.png binary
*.icns binary
*.ico binary
